# Web 1, Web 2 & Web 3 - How the evolution of the web shaped the emergence of Blockchain Technology

## Early Internet

The printing press was perhaps the biggest innovation, enabling enhanced communication between masses until the telegraph came along. The telegraph was followed by the radio, which could now broadcast trendy music, the latest news, tele-commercials and much more. But how great is a radio compared to a television? Now, images and videos can also be transmitted across vast distances. Alongside all this, a powerful technology had been developing side-by-side that rolled up all of media - writing, reading, music, images and videos into one single medium.

The first group of networked computers communicated with each other in 1969, and ARPANET, or the Advanced Research Projects Agency Network became the start of the internet. The early internet era began around the 1970s. At that point in time, not everyone could access computers. It was reserved for governmental, military and high business operations. The majority of the internet was used for e-mail discussions. From the 1970s to the 1980s,

In 1984, the DNS (Domain Name System) was created, allowing the organization and classification of the world’s online sites. This address system is still in use today; for example, .com, .org, .edu.

## Evolution of the Web

### Web 1 or “static web”

The era of web 1 spans from the 1990s to the early 2000s. Web 1 is also called the “Static web” because it contained read-only information portals where users could not interact with the information. These sites provided information without interactive elements or user-generated content. This was the “read-only” era. Examples include Geocities, Netscape Navigator and the Internet Movie Database (IMDb).

Users were unable to write their own content onto the internet and it was devoid of write-read-write interactions such as the ones we have these days. No option to comment, only read the posts.

### Web 2 or the “dynamic web”

The era of web 2 spans from the 2000s to the present. This is the “read-and-write” era. Users are able to post information and interact with existing content. Centralized control however, lies with big corporations like Google and Microsoft. Examples of web 2 include Facebook, Instagram and X.

### Web 3 or the “decentralized web”

The era of Web3 is the next evolution, often called the read-write-own web, built on blockchain technology. This is the “read, write and own” era. Core features of web 3 are decentralization, user ownership, and control over data & digital assets. Web3 aims to eliminate corporate intermediaries and reduce censorship though it is still emerging.

Web3 emphasizes the transformative potential of blockchain technology. Web3 restores the internet’s original promise of decentralization and individual digital empowerment. Web3 harnesses blockchain networks to cultivate authenticity, creativity & entrepreneurship.

## Blockchain Technology

Blockchain allows members to collectively govern the system without the interference of platform owners as observed in Web 2 / most sites we use presently. It facilitates interactions and transactions with pre-coded algorithms (or smart contracts), which have conditions to set the rules for all members. Such a decentralized structure engages users in decision-making, allowing fair evaluations of individual interests.
